["create","DOCS-S3-T1",1780750802,"codex-worker",{"title":"Write docs/harness-hook.md integration guide","parent":"DOCS-S3","type":"task","scope":["docs/harness-hook.md (new)","docs/commands.md"],"acceptance":["docs/harness-hook.md exists","What-it-does section explains scope and acceptance enforcement","Required env vars ARMATURE_TASK_ID and ARMATURE_HOOK_PLATFORM documented","Claude Code .claude/settings.json hooks JSON block present and exact","Codex codex.toml hooks TOML block present and exact","Test command for verifying hook integration present","docs/commands.md harness-hook section links to guide"],"definition_of_done":"Write docs/harness-hook.md explaining the end-to-end harness-hook integration. Cover: (1) What it does — intercepts agent tool calls (PreToolUse events for Edit, Write, MultiEdit, Bash) and stop events; checks them against the active task's scope and acceptance criteria; returns allow or block decisions; (2) Required runtime env vars — ARMATURE_TASK_ID (the issue ID of the active task, set before launching each agent) and ARMATURE_HOOK_PLATFORM (one of: claude, codex, devin); (3) Claude Code setup — no installer command exists; manually add to .claude/settings.json the hooks block shown below (exact JSON must be in the guide): PreToolUse with matcher Edit|Write|MultiEdit|Bash calling arm harness-hook, and Stop calling arm harness-hook; (4) Codex setup — manually add to codex.toml the [hooks] section with pre_tool_use = \"arm harness-hook\" and stop = \"arm harness-hook\" (exact TOML must be in the guide); (5) Devin setup — note that Devin uses a structured hook event format; consult internal/harnesshook/platform_devin.go for the current config format since no installer command exists; (6) Testing — verify the hook is active by running `echo '{\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":true}' | ARMATURE_TASK_ID=\u003cissue-id\u003e ARMATURE_HOOK_PLATFORM=claude arm harness-hook` and confirming a JSON decision response. Update docs/commands.md harness-hook section to link to this guide.","priority":"medium","confidence":"draft"}]
["create","DOCS-S3-T2",1780750802,"codex-worker",{"title":"Write docs/concepts.md agent reference for core system model","parent":"DOCS-S3","type":"task","scope":["docs/concepts.md (new)","README.md"],"acceptance":["docs/concepts.md exists","All 8 concepts present","Each concept contains at least one concrete command or rule agents can act on","README.md Documentation section links to docs/concepts.md"],"definition_of_done":"Write docs/concepts.md as a reference for agents that need to understand the Armature system model to make correct decisions in edge cases. State each concept as operational rules, not narrative. Cover 8 concepts: (1) Ops log and materialization — each worker appends to its own JSONL file at .armature/ops/\u003cworker-id\u003e.log; state files under .armature/state/ are derived caches rebuilt by replay and are never source of truth; never edit ops files directly; (2) Worker identity — registered via `arm worker-init`, stored in git config key armature.worker-id, unique UUID per clone; re-running without --check generates a new UUID; use `arm worker-init --check || arm worker-init` idiom; (3) Claim lifecycle — claim via `arm claim`; default TTL 60 minutes; send `arm heartbeat \u003cid\u003e` (max 1/min) to keep alive; a claim is lost if TTL expires without heartbeat; in dual-branch mode downstream tasks require merged not done to unblock; (4) DAG hierarchy — valid parent-child types: epic can parent story, task, bug; story can parent task, bug; task and bug cannot parent anything; this is enforced by E5 validation; (5) Citations and validation — every issue must have a citation before `arm validate` passes; two paths: `arm source-link` (links to a registered source document) or `arm accept-citation` (self-certifies with rationale); `arm validate --ci` exits non-zero on any error; (6) Confidence levels — `decompose-apply` creates issues in draft state; draft issues are invisible to the ready queue; promote with `arm dag-transition --issue \u003croot-id\u003e` before workers can claim; (7) Single vs dual-branch — single-branch: .armature/ lives on main branch, tasks go directly done→complete; dual-branch: .armature/ on orphan _armature branch, .arm/ worktree for ops writes, downstream tasks require merged not done; (8) Source documents and decomposition — register sources via `arm sources add`; `arm decompose-context --sources all` packages source content for an AI agent to analyze; `arm decompose-apply --plan plan.json` loads the resulting task DAG. Add a Documentation section to README.md listing the docs/ files including a link to docs/concepts.md.","priority":"medium","confidence":"draft"}]
